-- title:
--   Walsh norms of the identity tend to zero under cotype and approximation
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. A normed space $Z$ satisfies the fixed cotype bound of length $m$ with constant $D$ if $\bigl(\sum_{i\le m}\|z_i\|^2\bigr)^{1/2}\le D\bigl(\mathbb E\|\sum_{i\le m}\varepsilon_iz_i\|^2\bigr)^{1/2}$ for all $z_1,\dots,z_m\in Z$. The Walsh norm $\omega_n(A)$ of an operator $A$ is the norm of $f\mapsto(v\mapsto\mathbb E_u\chi_v(u)Af(u))$ on $L_2(\{0,1\}^n)$-valued functions. A Banach space $X$ has the approximation property if on every compact set the identity can be uniformly approximated by finite-rank operators. Let $X$ be a real Banach space with the approximation property. Suppose $X^*$ and $X$ satisfy the fixed cotype bounds of length $m$ with constants $D_*\ge1$ and $D\ge1$. With $d=\lceil2\log_2m\rceil$, assume $4\le d<m$ and
--   $$
--   80\,d(d+1)^2\le\min\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt m}{48\cdot12D_*},\ \frac{\sqrt m}{48D}\Bigr).
--   $$
--   Then $\omega_N(\mathrm{id}_X)\to0$ as $N\to\infty$.
--
--   The Walsh transform on $L_2(\{0,1\}^N;X)$ becomes uniformly small, which rules out uniform copies of $\ell_1^n$.
